WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.042 --> 00:00:15.309
Everyone has a story,

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:18.268
and that story is filled with chapters

00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:20.833
that have made us who we are today.

00:00:22.458 --> 00:00:25.684
Those early chapters of that story

00:00:25.708 --> 00:00:29.601
sometimes are the ones
that define us the most.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:32.309
The Center for Disease Control

00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:36.101
has estimated that over half
of our nation's children

00:00:36.125 --> 00:00:40.333
have experienced at least
one or two types of childhood trauma.

00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:44.792
That adversity can have lasting effects.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:49.184
When I began to have
opportunities to speak

00:00:49.208 --> 00:00:52.518
and advocate for students
and for teachers,

00:00:52.542 --> 00:00:55.768
I found myself uniquely positioned

00:00:55.792 --> 00:00:58.750
to be able to speak
about childhood trauma.

00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:01.976
But I had to make a decision first.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.768
I had to decide,

00:01:03.792 --> 00:01:07.351
did I want to just share
the bright and shiny parts of my life,

00:01:07.375 --> 00:01:10.309
you know, those ones
that we put out on social media

00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:13.018
that make us all look perfect,

00:01:13.042 --> 00:01:17.684
or did I want to make myself vulnerable

00:01:17.708 --> 00:01:19.292
and become an open book?

00:01:20.458 --> 00:01:23.101
The choice became very clear.

00:01:23.125 --> 00:01:25.934
In order to make a difference
in the life of a child,

00:01:25.958 --> 00:01:29.809
I had to become transparent.

00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:33.625
So I made the commitment
to tell my personal story.

00:01:34.625 --> 00:01:38.934
And this story is filled
with people that have loved me

00:01:38.958 --> 00:01:41.768
and taken care of me and grown me.

00:01:41.792 --> 00:01:45.351
And have helped me overcome and heal.

00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:48.875
And now it's time for me
to help others do the same.

00:01:51.958 --> 00:01:55.208
When I first started school,
I was the picture of normalcy.

00:01:56.042 --> 00:01:58.643
I was from a good family,

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.018
I was always dressed nicely,

00:02:01.042 --> 00:02:03.351
had a smile on my face,

00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:05.417
I was prepared for school.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.893
But my life was anything but normal.

00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:14.833
By this time, I had already become
a victim of sexual abuse.

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:18.042
And it was still happening.

00:02:19.083 --> 00:02:21.309
My parents didn't know,

00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.083
and I had not told anyone else.

00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:31.851
When I started school, I felt like
this was going to be my safe place.

00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:34.268
So I was excited.

00:02:34.292 --> 00:02:39.083
Imagine my dismay when I met my teacher,

00:02:40.542 --> 00:02:42.851
Mr. Randolph.

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:46.643
Now Mr. Randolph was not my abuser.

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:50.226
But Mr. Randolph was the epitome

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.333
of everything that scared me
the most in my life.

00:02:55.333 --> 00:02:59.434
I had already started
these self-preservation techniques

00:02:59.458 --> 00:03:05.351
to where I took myself out of positions
where I was going to be alone with a man.

00:03:05.375 --> 00:03:09.143
And here I was, as a student,

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:12.393
I was going to be in a classroom
with a man every day,

00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:14.875
for a year of school.

00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:19.000
I was scared; I didn't trust him.

00:03:20.125 --> 00:03:21.393
But you know what,

00:03:21.417 --> 00:03:24.375
Mr. Randolph would turn out
to be my greatest advocate.

00:03:25.375 --> 00:03:27.143
But in the beginning,

00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:30.893
oh, I made sure he knew
I did not like him.

00:03:30.917 --> 00:03:33.268
I was noncompliant;

00:03:33.292 --> 00:03:36.875
I was that kid that was disengaged.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:42.143
And I also made it really hard
on my parents, too.

00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:43.643
I didn't want to go to school,

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:47.143
so I fought them every morning,
getting on the bus.

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:48.893
At night, I couldn't sleep,

00:03:48.917 --> 00:03:52.059
because my anxiety was so high.

00:03:52.083 --> 00:03:55.726
So I was going into class exhausted.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:04:00.226
Which, exhausted children
are cranky children,

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.976
and they're not easy to teach,

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.250
you know that.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:08.851
Mr. Randolph could have
approached me with frustration,

00:04:08.875 --> 00:04:11.958
like so many teachers do
with students like me.

00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:15.476
But not him.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.184
He approached me with empathy

00:04:19.208 --> 00:04:21.042
and with flexibility.

00:04:21.917 --> 00:04:24.768
I was so grateful for that.

00:04:24.792 --> 00:04:29.684
He saw this six-year-old
was tired and weary.

00:04:29.708 --> 00:04:32.559
And so instead of making me
go outside for recess,

00:04:32.583 --> 00:04:34.851
he would let me stay in and take naps,

00:04:34.875 --> 00:04:37.667
because he knew I needed rest.

00:04:39.208 --> 00:04:42.851
Instead of sitting
at the teacher table at lunch,

00:04:42.875 --> 00:04:46.309
he would come and sit with the students
at the student table.

00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:50.958
He would engage me
and all my classmates in conversation.

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:54.351
And I now look back and I know

00:04:54.375 --> 00:04:55.768
he had a purpose for that,

00:04:55.792 --> 00:04:59.101
he was listening, he was asking questions.

00:04:59.125 --> 00:05:02.000
He needed to find out what was going on.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.893
He built a relationship with me.

00:05:05.917 --> 00:05:08.559
He earned my trust.

00:05:08.583 --> 00:05:09.851
And slowly but surely,

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:12.434
those walls that I had built around myself

00:05:12.458 --> 00:05:14.393
he started chipping away at,

00:05:14.417 --> 00:05:18.000
and I eventually realized
he was one of the good guys.

00:05:20.917 --> 00:05:25.792
I know that he felt like he wasn't enough.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.518
Because he made the move
to talk to my mom.

00:05:31.542 --> 00:05:33.601
And got my mom's permission

00:05:33.625 --> 00:05:37.268
to let me start seeing
a school guidance counselor,

00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:38.833
Ms. McFadyen.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.809
I started seeing Ms. McFadyen
once or twice a week

00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:44.934
for the next two years.

00:05:44.958 --> 00:05:46.542
It was a process.

00:05:47.875 --> 00:05:49.143
During that time period,

00:05:49.167 --> 00:05:51.518
I never disclosed to her the abuse,

00:05:51.542 --> 00:05:54.184
because it was a secret;

00:05:54.208 --> 00:05:56.434
I wasn't supposed to tell.

00:05:56.458 --> 00:05:58.976
But she connected the dots,
I know she did,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.393
because everything that she did with me

00:06:02.417 --> 00:06:05.875
was to empower me
and help me find my voice.

00:06:06.958 --> 00:06:09.684
She taught me how to use mental images

00:06:09.708 --> 00:06:12.559
to push through my fears.

00:06:12.583 --> 00:06:14.601
She taught me breathing techniques

00:06:14.625 --> 00:06:17.393
to help me get through
those anxiety attacks

00:06:17.417 --> 00:06:20.059
that I would have so often.

00:06:20.083 --> 00:06:22.125
And she role-played with me.

00:06:23.042 --> 00:06:24.309
And she made sure

00:06:24.333 --> 00:06:28.417
that I could stand up
for myself in situations.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:30.809
And the day came

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:33.351
where I was in the room with my abuser

00:06:33.375 --> 00:06:35.851
and one other adult.

00:06:35.875 --> 00:06:37.583
And I told my truth.

00:06:39.208 --> 00:06:41.875
I told about the abuse.

00:06:42.792 --> 00:06:46.851
Immediately, my abuser began to deny,

00:06:46.875 --> 00:06:49.893
and the person I disclosed to,

00:06:49.917 --> 00:06:52.684
they just weren't equipped
to handle the bombshell

00:06:52.708 --> 00:06:55.559
that I had just dropped on them.

00:06:55.583 --> 00:06:59.226
It was easier to believe the abuser

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.875
rather than a child.

00:07:02.208 --> 00:07:06.125
So I was told never to speak of it again.

00:07:07.167 --> 00:07:11.125
I was made to feel like I had done
something wrong, again.

00:07:13.375 --> 00:07:15.375
It was devastating.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.518
But you know what,

00:07:18.542 --> 00:07:20.393
something good came out of that day.

00:07:20.417 --> 00:07:23.833
My abuser knew that I was no longer
going to be silent.

00:07:25.125 --> 00:07:27.226
The power shifted.

00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:30.601
And the abuse stopped.

00:07:30.625 --> 00:07:37.476
(Applause)

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.684
But the shame

00:07:39.708 --> 00:07:43.101
and fear of it happening again

00:07:43.125 --> 00:07:44.559
remained.

00:07:44.583 --> 00:07:46.768
And it would remain with me

00:07:46.792 --> 00:07:49.458
for many, many years to come.

00:07:51.583 --> 00:07:54.893
Mr. Randolph and Ms. McFadyen,

00:07:54.917 --> 00:07:57.583
they helped me find my voice.

00:07:59.875 --> 00:08:04.125
They helped me find the light out.

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:06.934
But you know what,

00:08:06.958 --> 00:08:10.101
there are so many kids
that aren't as fortunate as me.

00:08:10.125 --> 00:08:13.184
And you have them in your classrooms.

00:08:13.208 --> 00:08:16.809
That is why it's so important for me
to talk to you today,

00:08:16.833 --> 00:08:18.934
so you can be aware

00:08:18.958 --> 00:08:22.851
and you can start asking the questions
that need to be asked

00:08:22.875 --> 00:08:25.809
and paying attention to these students,

00:08:25.833 --> 00:08:30.042
so you too can help them find their way.

00:08:31.583 --> 00:08:33.643
As a kindergarten teacher,

00:08:33.667 --> 00:08:35.559
I start my year off

00:08:35.583 --> 00:08:40.184
with my kids making box biographies.

00:08:40.208 --> 00:08:42.601
These are two of my students.

00:08:42.625 --> 00:08:44.518
And I encourage them

00:08:44.542 --> 00:08:48.268
to fill those boxes with things
that tell me about them

00:08:48.292 --> 00:08:49.559
and about their life,

00:08:49.583 --> 00:08:52.476
what's important to you, you know?

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:54.059
They decorate them,

00:08:54.083 --> 00:08:56.226
I mean, they really take time,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.768
they fill them with pictures
of their families and of their pets,

00:09:00.792 --> 00:09:04.458
and then I let them present them
to me and to the class.

00:09:05.333 --> 00:09:08.417
And during that time,
I am an active listener.

00:09:09.417 --> 00:09:11.893
Because the things they say,

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:15.018
the facial expressions that they give me,

00:09:15.042 --> 00:09:18.684
the things they don't say

00:09:18.708 --> 00:09:21.309
can become red flags for me

00:09:21.333 --> 00:09:25.101
and can help me figure out
what their needs are.

00:09:25.125 --> 00:09:27.893
What is driving them

00:09:27.917 --> 00:09:31.375
to maybe have the behaviors
that they're showing me in class.

00:09:32.375 --> 00:09:34.768
How can I be a better teacher

00:09:34.792 --> 00:09:36.917
by listening to their voices?

00:09:38.083 --> 00:09:41.434
I also make times to develop
relationships with them,

00:09:41.458 --> 00:09:43.768
much like Mr. Randolph did with me.

00:09:43.792 --> 00:09:45.351
I sit with them at lunch,

00:09:45.375 --> 00:09:48.309
I have conversations with them at recess,

00:09:48.333 --> 00:09:50.976
I go to their games on the weekends,

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.101
I go to their dance recitals.

00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:55.559
I become a part of their life.

00:09:55.583 --> 00:09:58.976
Because in order to really know a student,

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:02.042
you've got to infuse yourself
into their lives.

00:10:03.458 --> 00:10:06.226
Now I know some of you
are middle school teachers

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:08.101
and high school teachers,

00:10:08.125 --> 00:10:10.893
and you might think that those kids

00:10:10.917 --> 00:10:14.143
have already kind of
developed, and you know,

00:10:14.167 --> 00:10:17.018
they're on autopilot at that point.

00:10:17.042 --> 00:10:18.893
But don't be deceived.

00:10:18.917 --> 00:10:21.559
Especially the kids that you think
have it all together,

00:10:21.583 --> 00:10:26.018
because those are the ones
that might need you the most.

00:10:26.042 --> 00:10:27.809
If you were to look at my yearbook,

00:10:27.833 --> 00:10:30.059
you would see me on about every page,

00:10:30.083 --> 00:10:34.476
because I was involved in everything.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.143
I even drove a school bus.

00:10:36.167 --> 00:10:37.559
(Laughs)

00:10:37.583 --> 00:10:39.351
So I was that kid

00:10:39.375 --> 00:10:42.101
that teachers thought
was the overachiever,

00:10:42.125 --> 00:10:43.643
the popular person,

00:10:43.667 --> 00:10:46.042
the one that had it together.

00:10:46.958 --> 00:10:49.976
But guys, I was lost.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.393
I was lost,

00:10:51.417 --> 00:10:54.393
and I wanted someone to ask me,

00:10:54.417 --> 00:10:56.518
"Lisa, why are you here all the time,

00:10:56.542 --> 00:11:00.476
why are you throwing yourself
into all these things?"

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.684
Did they ever wonder,

00:11:02.708 --> 00:11:04.976
was I running away from someone,

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.893
was I running away from something?

00:11:07.917 --> 00:11:11.726
Why did I not want to be in my community

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.184
or in my home?

00:11:13.208 --> 00:11:16.000
Why did I want to be
at school all the time?

00:11:17.375 --> 00:11:19.458
No one ever asked.

00:11:20.958 --> 00:11:22.518
Now don't get me wrong,

00:11:22.542 --> 00:11:24.726
all overachievers in your schools

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:28.226
are not victims of abuse or trauma.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:33.226
But I just want you
to take the time to be curious.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:35.458
Ask them why.

00:11:36.458 --> 00:11:40.184
You may find out
that there is a reason behind it.

00:11:40.208 --> 00:11:45.476
You could be the reason
that they move forward

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.083
with their story.

00:11:49.208 --> 00:11:51.684
Be careful not to assume

00:11:51.708 --> 00:11:54.601
that you already know
the ending to their story.

00:11:54.625 --> 00:11:58.309
Don't put a period
where a semicolon should be.

00:11:58.333 --> 00:11:59.851
Keep that story going

00:11:59.875 --> 00:12:04.476
and help them know that even if
something has happened traumatic to them,

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:07.125
that their life is still worth telling.

00:12:08.208 --> 00:12:11.250
Their story is worth telling.

00:12:13.625 --> 00:12:15.934
Now in order to do that,

00:12:15.958 --> 00:12:21.000
I really feel like we have to embrace
our own personal stories as educators.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:24.059
Many of you might be sitting there

00:12:24.083 --> 00:12:26.333
and thinking, "Yeah.

00:12:27.542 --> 00:12:29.476
That happened to me.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.143
But I'm not ready to share."

00:12:32.167 --> 00:12:33.583
And that's OK.

00:12:34.667 --> 00:12:36.518
The time will come

00:12:36.542 --> 00:12:39.559
when you will feel it inside your soul

00:12:39.583 --> 00:12:43.101
that it's time to turn your past pain

00:12:43.125 --> 00:12:45.893
into purpose for the future.

00:12:45.917 --> 00:12:48.375
These children are our future.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.393
I just encourage you
to take it day by day.

00:12:53.417 --> 00:12:55.559
Talk to someone.

00:12:55.583 --> 00:12:58.292
Be willing and just open.

00:13:00.042 --> 00:13:03.601
My life story came full circle

00:13:03.625 --> 00:13:06.643
in the spring of 2018,

00:13:06.667 --> 00:13:08.309
where I was invited to speak

00:13:08.333 --> 00:13:11.976
to a group of beginning
teachers and mentors.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.268
I shared my story,
much like today with you,

00:13:15.292 --> 00:13:18.167
and afterwards I had a lady approach me.

00:13:19.333 --> 00:13:24.184
She had tears in her eyes
and she quietly said, "Thank you.

00:13:24.208 --> 00:13:26.643
Thank you for sharing.

00:13:26.667 --> 00:13:30.559
I cannot wait to tell my dad

00:13:30.583 --> 00:13:32.625
everything that I heard today."

00:13:33.625 --> 00:13:37.101
She must have seen
the perplexed look on my face,

00:13:37.125 --> 00:13:40.101
because she followed up by saying,

00:13:40.125 --> 00:13:42.309
"Mr. Randolph is my dad."

00:13:42.333 --> 00:13:44.643
Audience: Aww.

00:13:44.667 --> 00:13:48.559
Lisa Godwin: "And he often wonders:

00:13:48.583 --> 00:13:50.333
Did he make a difference?

00:13:51.833 --> 00:13:53.976
Today, I get to go home and tell him,

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:56.667
'You definitely made a difference.'"

00:13:57.833 --> 00:13:59.559
What a gift.

00:13:59.583 --> 00:14:01.601
What a gift.

00:14:01.625 --> 00:14:03.184
And that prompted me

00:14:03.208 --> 00:14:06.226
to reach out to Ms. McFadyen's
daughter as well,

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:08.184
and to share with her

00:14:08.208 --> 00:14:11.518
what an impact Ms. McFadyen had made.

00:14:11.542 --> 00:14:13.351
And I wanted her to know

00:14:13.375 --> 00:14:16.226
I have advocated for more funding

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:19.476
for guidance counselors,
for school social workers,

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.393
for psychologists, for nurses,

00:14:21.417 --> 00:14:25.851
because they are so vital
to the mental and physical health

00:14:25.875 --> 00:14:27.726
of our children.

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:29.726
I'm thankful for Ms. McFadyen.

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:34.143
(Applause)

00:14:34.167 --> 00:14:36.934
I once heard someone say,

00:14:36.958 --> 00:14:39.643
in order to find your way
out of the darkness,

00:14:39.667 --> 00:14:41.792
you have to find the light.

00:14:42.792 --> 00:14:45.976
Today, I hope that you leave this place

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.268
and you seek opportunities
to be the light.

00:14:50.292 --> 00:14:52.018
For not only students

00:14:52.042 --> 00:14:55.226
but for adults in your classrooms,

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.583
in your schools, in your communities.

00:14:58.672 --> 00:15:01.643
You have the gift

00:15:01.667 --> 00:15:04.042
to help someone navigate

00:15:05.292 --> 00:15:07.809
through their trauma

00:15:07.833 --> 00:15:11.934
and make their story worth telling.

00:15:11.958 --> 00:15:13.226
Thank you.

00:15:13.250 --> 00:15:19.125
(Applause)

